<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="salicylate glucosides biosynthesis III" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6624" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-110 &quot;Salicylic acid&quot;| (salicylate; SA) is an aromatic compound commonly found throughout the plant kingdom that is also found in some bacteria. In plants |FRAME: CPD-110 SA| affects numerous biological and physiological processes, including plant defense (via the systematic acquired resistance system |CITS: [15283665][12873532]|), allelopathy, flowering, and stomatal movement |CITS: [SCHULZ93]|. Given its important signaling role, plants often chemically modify |FRAME: CPD-110 SA| by attaching glycosyl, methyl, or other groups to |FRAME: CPD-110 SA| to alters its activity, volatility, or other properties |CITS [PIERPOINT94][SCHULZ93]|. One of the potential glucose-conjugates |FRAME: CPD-12629| (SGE), has been detected in a number of plant species, including |FRAME: ORG-5993| |CITS: [18248508]|, |FRAME: TAX-4097|, |FRAME: TAX-4075|, and &lt;i&gt;Thlaspi goesingense&lt;/i&gt;|CITS: [15734913]|. The specific biological role of |FRAME: CPD-12629 SGE| is still under investigation. Unlike the other SA glucose, |FRAME: CPD-12628| (SAG), SGE is not expected to be a more water soluable form of the compound, and there is evidence that it is not accepted by the vacuolar transporters that require a negative charge |CITS: [15032822][15871031]|. SGE may serve as a high energy intermediate form of |FRAME: CPD-110 SA| that can be used in additional biochemical pathways, or, it may be another storage form of this important signaling compound |CITS: [18248508]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The formation of |FRAME: CPD-12629 SGE| has been demonstrated &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; using glucosyltransferases in several plant species, including |FRAME: TAX-35883| |CITS: [17692286]|, |FRAME: TAX-4097| |CITS: [10593966]|, and |FRAME: ORG-5993| |CITS: [18248508]|. In general, the enzymes from these organisms do not have high substrate specificity which may be a beneficial trait that allows the enzymes to detoxify numerous potential xenobiotics.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="dreher" />
  </Authors>
